"use strict";(self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara=self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara||[]).push([["1778"],{94515(s){s.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"FIN-210","slug":"worst-case-scenario","term":"Worst-Case Scenario","also_known_as":["Downside Case"],"difficulty":"Beginner","definition":"The worst-case scenario is a forecast based on pessimistic but still plausible assumptions, showing how bad results could be if things go wrong.","explanation":["A worst-case scenario combines unfavourable assumptions, such as falling sales, lower prices, rising costs or the loss of a major customer, to show the lower range of likely outcomes.","For example, if a company\'s base case forecasts profit of 4 million, its worst case might assume a recession, a 20% fall in sales and higher interest costs, leading to a loss of 1 million.","The main purpose is risk management. Seeing the worst case helps a business decide whether it could survive a bad year, how much cash it should hold, whether its debt is too high and what actions it would take if conditions deteriorated.","A worst-case scenario is not the same as a stress test. The worst case is usually a plausible bad outcome, while a stress test may examine more extreme shocks. Both help decision-makers avoid being caught unprepared."],"note":"A downside case is very similar to a worst-case scenario. Some analysts use \\"downside case\\" for a moderately pessimistic outcome and keep \\"worst case\\" for the most severe plausible one.","total":1500,"prev":{"slug":"world-bank","term":"World Bank"},"next":{"slug":"write-off","term":"Write-Off"}}')}}]);